<exercisegroup>
<title>Numerically Given Linear Functions</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Decide if the given table could represent a linear function and explain your reasoning.  For each table that could represent a linear function, find a formula for that function.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>x</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>y</m></cell>
<cell><m>12.6</m></cell>
<cell><m>9.3</m></cell>
<cell><m>6.0</m></cell>
<cell><m>2.7</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Notice that <m>x</m> changes by <m>+1</m> moving left to right, and <m>y</m> changes by <m>-3.3</m>. Therefore, the slope between each pair of consecutive points is always <m>\frac{-3.3}{1} =-3.3</m>, and because this is the same number for all, this is a linear function.

Its slope is <m>-3.3</m> and its vertical intercept is <m>12.6</m> (from the table) so
<md>
y = -3.3x + 12.6.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>




<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>t</m></cell>
<cell><m>-1</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>5</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>f(t)</m></cell>
<cell><m>0.1</m></cell>
<cell><m>0.4</m></cell>
<cell><m>0.7</m></cell>
<cell><m>1.0</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
The change in <m>t</m> (the <q>run</q>) is always <m>2</m>, and the change in <m>f(t)</m> (the <q>rise</q>) is always <m>0.3</m>, and so this is a linear function with slope <m>m=\frac{0.3}{2} = 0.15</m>.
</p>

<p>
To find the equation, either use point-slope form which would look something like
<md>
f(t)=0.15(t-(-1))+0.1 = 0.15(t+1)+0.1
</md> or use slope-intercept form and solve for <m>b</m>. That is,
<md>
f(-1) = 0.1 = 0.15(-1) + b \quad \implies \quad b = 0.25
</md> and so <m>f(t)=0.15t + 0.25</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>




<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>n</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>1.5</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>4.5</m></cell>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>g(n)</m></cell>
<cell><m>12</m></cell>
<cell><m>18</m></cell>
<cell><m>12</m></cell>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
<cell><m>12</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
The change in <m>n</m> (the <q>run</q>) is always <m>1.5</m>, but the change in <m>g(n)</m> (the <q>rise</q>) changes <mdash/> it is <m>6</m>, <m>-6</m>, <m>-6</m>, and then <m>6</m>. Because these are not always the same number, the slopes between consecutive points are not equal, and so the function is nonlinear.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>t</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
<cell><m>8</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>h(t)</m></cell>
<cell><m>6.4</m></cell>
<cell><m>1.6</m></cell>
<cell><m>0.4</m></cell>
<cell><m>0.1</m></cell>
<cell><m>0.025</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The change in <m>t</m> (the <q>run</q>) is always <m>2</m>, but the change in <m>h(t)</m> (the <q>rise</q>) changes; it is <m>-4.4</m>, <m>-1.2</m>, <m>-0.3</m>, and then <m>-0.0075</m>. Because these are not always the same number, the slopes between consecutive points are not equal, and so the function is nonlinear.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise>
<statement>
<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>z</m></cell>
<cell><m>-2</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
<cell><m>7</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>w(z)</m></cell>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
<cell><m>4.8</m></cell>
<cell><m>3.6</m></cell>
<cell><m>2.4</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The change in <m>z</m> is always <m>3</m>, and the change in <m>w(z)</m> is always <m>-1.2</m> and so this is a linear function. The slope is <m>m = \frac{-1.2}{3} = -0.4</m>, and we can find the equation using point-slope form such as
<md>
w(z) = -0.4(z-(-2)) + 6 = -0.4(z+2)+6
</md> or use slope-intercept form and solve for <m>b</m>, such as
<md>
w(z) = -0.4z + b \quad \implies \quad 6 = -0.4(-2) + b \quad \implies \quad b = 5.2.
</md> And therefore <m>w(z) = -0.4z + 5.2</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



</exercisegroup>




<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
The height of a human individual can be estimated by the length of the femur, as shown for males in the following table.
</p>
<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>L</m> (length of femur in cm)</cell>
<cell><m>42</m></cell>
<cell><m>45</m></cell>
<cell><m>48</m></cell>
<cell><m>51</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>H</m> (height of human male in cm)</cell>
<cell><m>162.97</m></cell>
<cell><m>169.93</m></cell>
<cell><m>176.89</m></cell>
<cell><m>183.85</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Use the information in this table to find a possible formula for the height of a human male <m>H</m> as a function of his femur length <m>L</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Note that the change in <m>L</m> is <m>3</m>, and the change in <m>H</m> is always <m>6.96</m> and so this is a linear function with slope <m>m = \frac{6.96}{3}  = 2.32</m>. Now, either use point-slope form or slope-intercept form to obtain the equation for the function. Using slope-intercept form, then
<md>
H = 2.32L + b.
</md> Plugging in any point, say <m>(42,162.97)</m>, results in
<md>
162.97 = 2.32 (42) + b
</md> meaning that <m>b = 65.53</m> and so
<md>
H = 2.32L + 65.53.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
What would the approximate height of a human male with femur length <m>46</m> cm be?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
By plugging in <m>L = 46</m>, then the approximate height is
<md>
H = 2.32(46) + 65.53 = 172.23 \text{ cm} .
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task>
<statement>
<p>
Fernando is <m>175.4</m> cm tall. What would you expect the approximate length of his femur to be?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
With a height of <m>H = 175.4</m>, then
<md>
175.4 = 2.32L + 65.53.
</md> Solving for <m>L</m>, we get
<md>
109.87 = 2.32 L \quad \implies \quad L = 47.36 \text{ cm} .
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>


</exercise>




</worksheet>